    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How wide does a concrete crack have to be before I should worry?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "On a driveway or patio, width alone is rarely alarming; offset and growth matter more. On a foundation wall, cracks wider than about 1/4 in, horizontal cracks, or stair-step cracks in block deserve an engineer's look. A hairline crack that has not changed in a year is almost always shrinkage and only needs sealing to keep water out."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I use regular silicone caulk to repair concrete cracks?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It is not a good choice. Silicone does not bond as well to concrete as polyurethane, it cannot be painted or sealed over, and most general-purpose silicones are not rated for horizontal traffic. Polyurethane or hybrid polymer sealants labeled for concrete are designed for the movement and abrasion that a driveway or walkway crack sees, and they cost about the same."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Should I fill control joints in my driveway?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, but only with a flexible sealant, never with mortar or epoxy. Control joints are there to let the slab move and crack in a straight line. Filling them with a rigid material defeats their purpose. A flexible polyurethane over backer rod keeps water and weeds out while letting the joint keep doing its job."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does concrete crack repair last?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A well-prepped polyurethane sealant in a moving crack commonly lasts five to ten years before it needs to be cut out and replaced, according to typical manufacturer guidance. Liquid crack sealers in hairline cracks often need touching up every couple of years. Epoxy in a truly dormant interior crack can last as long as the slab itself."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I repair concrete cracks in winter?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually not outdoors. Most sealants need the concrete at 40 F or above and rising, and cement patches cannot freeze in their first day or two. If a crack is letting water into the base before winter, a temporary fill is better than nothing, but plan to cut it out and redo it properly in spring when temperatures are in the label range."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do I need to grind or route a crack before filling it?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not always. Clean cracks with sound, square edges between 1/8 in and 1/2 in can be filled after brushing and vacuuming. Routing helps when a crack is a ragged hairline that sealant cannot get into, or when the faces are crumbly and weak. A crack-chasing blade on an angle grinder turns it into a clean groove sealant can grip."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Will crack repair stop a crack from coming back?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It stops water and debris from getting in, which slows down deterioration, but it does not stop the slab from moving. A flexible sealant moves with the crack, so the crack stays sealed rather than disappearing. If the cause is settlement, heave or corroding steel, the crack will widen regardless of the filler until that cause is addressed."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
  ],
};
