        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="read-the-crack" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Read the Crack Before You Buy Anything
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Width</h3>

          <p>
            Start with width, because width decides the product family more than anything else. A hairline crack, roughly the thickness of a credit card or less, is usually plastic shrinkage from the first days of curing and rarely goes deeper than a fraction of an inch. A crack from about 1/16 in to 1/4 in wide is the range most liquid and self-leveling crack fillers are labeled for. Past 1/4 in, nearly every sealant manufacturer calls for a backer rod or a sand fill underneath, and past about 1/2 in you are often looking at patch mortar or a bigger conversation about why the slab cracked that wide.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Movement</h3>

          <p>
            Next, find out whether the crack moves. The simplest way is a pencil mark across the crack at each end and a strip of masking tape across the middle, dated. Check it after a hard freeze, a heat wave, or a few weeks of heavy rain. A crack that tears the tape or shifts the pencil marks is active. A crack that looks identical after a full season is dormant. Active cracks need a flexible sealant that stretches with them; dormant cracks can take a rigid filler like epoxy or a cement-based patch.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Offset</h3>

          <p>
            Then check whether the two sides line up. Run a straightedge or even a credit card across the crack. If one side sits higher than the other, the slab has settled or heaved, and that offset is a trip hazard that no filler will level. A vertical offset of about 1/4 in or more on a walkway is where most municipal sidewalk codes start calling it a defect, and it is a sign that the base under the concrete moved, not just the concrete itself.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pattern</h3>

          <p>
            Finally, look at the pattern. A single straight crack running across a driveway between control joints is classic shrinkage and is the least worrying crack there is. A web of fine cracks on the surface, called crazing or map cracking, is a finishing issue and is cosmetic. Cracks radiating from a corner or a post usually mean a stress point. Cracks that follow a buried line, such as a sewer trench or the old edge of a footing, point to a soft spot in the base. Each of these gets a different conversation, and only some of them end with a tube of sealant.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Reference Widths and Depths</p>
