        <div id="structural-signs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Structural Warning Signs That Change Everything
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Most Flatwork Cracks Are Not Structural</h3>

          <p>
            Most cracks in flatwork are not structural. Driveways, patios and sidewalks are slabs on grade, and they crack because concrete shrinks as it cures and nobody can stop that. Control joints exist to tell the crack where to go; when a crack forms somewhere else, it is usually because the joints were cut too shallow, too late, or too far apart. None of that threatens anything except appearance and water getting into the base.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Foundation Walls and Footings Are Different</h3>

          <p>
            Foundation walls, footings and anything that holds up part of a house are different. Horizontal cracks in a basement wall, stair-step cracks in block, a wall that bows inward, a crack wider at the top than the bottom, or any crack wider than about 1/4 in in a poured wall all deserve a structural engineer before a sealant. Our <a href="/concrete-wall-crack-repair/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">concrete wall crack repair</a> guide covers injection for the vertical, non-structural kind, and the <a href="/foundation-crack-repair-cost/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">foundation crack repair cost</a> guide covers what the structural fixes run.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Warning Signs on a Slab</h3>

          <p>
            On a slab, the warning signs are vertical displacement that keeps growing, cracks that reappear right next to a previous repair, sections that rock when you step on them, and voids you can hear: tap the slab with a hammer and a hollow drum sound means the base has washed out underneath. Filling the crack on a hollow slab buys a season at most. That slab needs to be lifted and the void filled, which is a <a href="/mudjacking-vs-polyjacking/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">mudjacking or polyjacking</a> job, or it needs to come out.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rust Stains and Corroding Steel</h3>

          <p>
            The other sign people miss is rust. A rust stain bleeding from a crack, or a crack running directly above where rebar or mesh sits, means steel is corroding. Corroding steel expands to several times its original volume and pushes the concrete apart from inside. Sealing the surface does nothing about the steel already rusting, and the crack will keep opening and eventually spall off chunks. That pattern belongs with the causes in our <a href="/concrete-popping-out/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">concrete popping out</a> guide.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">If the crack is wider, offset, or growing, find out why before you fill it.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A filler seals a crack. It does not stop settlement, heave, washout or rusting steel. When the cause is still active, the repair is the cause, and the crack filler is the last step, not the first.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="pick-the-method" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Pick the Method: Five Ways to Repair Concrete Cracks
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Liquid Crack Seal</h3>

          <p>
            Liquid crack seal is the easiest product and the most limited. It comes in a squeeze bottle, flows into hairline and narrow cracks up to roughly 1/4 in, and cures into a flexible rubbery bead. It is the right call for a sidewalk with a few thin cracks that you want to keep water out of. It is the wrong call for anything wide, deep or structural, and it tends to look like a dark line of tar unless you broadcast matching sand on top while it is wet.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Polyurethane Sealant Over Backer Rod</h3>

          <p>
            Polyurethane sealant over backer rod is the workhorse for moving cracks and joints from about 1/4 in up to around 1 in. It comes in two forms: gun-grade, which you tool with a putty knife and which works on vertical surfaces, and self-leveling, which you pour into horizontal cracks and let flow flat. Products like Sikaflex Self-Leveling Sealant and Quikrete Advanced Polymer Self-Leveling Sealant are the familiar names. Both stay flexible for years, which is the point, and both need a backer rod or sand fill so the sealant bonds to the two sides of the crack and not the bottom. Our guide to <a href="/self-leveling-concrete-crack-filler/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">self-leveling concrete crack filler</a> goes deep on that product type.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Two-Part Epoxy</h3>

          <p>
            Epoxy is for cracks that do not move. A two-part epoxy is far stronger than the concrete around it and bonds structurally, so it can glue a dormant crack back together. That same rigidity is why it fails on active cracks: if the slab moves, the epoxy holds and the concrete next to it breaks instead. Use epoxy for dormant cracks in garage floors before coating, for interior slabs that see no freeze-thaw, and for vertical wall cracks that an engineer has confirmed are stable.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Patch Mortar</h3>

          <p>
            Patch mortar, either a polymer-modified vinyl patch or a fast-setting repair mortar, is for cracks that have become gaps: wide, broken-edged, spalled or missing chunks. It is not flexible, so it is best on cracks that have stopped moving or on damaged edges next to a crack that you then seal separately. Routing, or chasing, is not a product but a step: you widen a ragged hairline crack with a grinder and crack-chasing blade into a clean groove, usually about 1/4 in wide and deep, so a sealant has two sound faces to bond to. Routing turns a crack that nothing sticks to into a joint that sealant was designed for.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Crack</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Moving?</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best method</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Avoid</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Hairline, under 1/16 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Liquid crack seal or penetrating sealer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Thick caulk smeared on top</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1/16 to 1/4 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Self-leveling polyurethane, no rod needed</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Epoxy, cement patch</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1/4 to 1 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Backer rod plus polyurethane sealant</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Filling full depth with sealant</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Any width</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No, interior</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Two-part epoxy</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Soft caulk if coating over it</td>
                  </tr>
