                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sealant alone</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Offset or hollow slab</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lift or replace, then seal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Any filler by itself</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Prep Is the Repair: Cleaning and Opening the Crack
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every sealant and epoxy label says the same thing in fine print: apply to clean, dry, sound concrete. That sentence is where most DIY repairs fail. A crack that has been open for a few years is packed with dirt, sand, weed roots, old sealant and a powdery layer of weak concrete along its faces. Sealant bonds to whatever it touches first, and if that is dust, the bead peels out in one strip the first time the crack opens.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Dig Out and Wire-Brush</h3>

          <p>
            Start by pulling weeds and digging out debris with a flat screwdriver, a 5-in-1 tool or a dedicated crack-cleaning hook. Then use a wire brush, ideally a narrow one that fits inside the crack, to scrub both faces. If the crack edges are ragged or the faces are smooth and glassy, run an angle grinder with a crack-chasing or diamond blade along it to cut fresh, square sides. Wear a respirator rated for silica dust and use a grinder with a shroud and vacuum if you can; concrete dust is crystalline silica and is not something to breathe.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Blow Out and Vacuum</h3>

          <p>
            Blow out the crack with compressed air or a leaf blower, then vacuum it with a shop vac and a crevice tool. Avoid pressure-washing right before sealing unless you can wait for the concrete to dry fully; most polyurethane sealants want a dry or at least surface-dry joint, and many specify no standing water. A day of sun after washing is usually enough for flatwork in summer. Epoxy is fussier, and most labels want the crack completely dry.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Set Backer Rod in Wide Cracks</h3>

          <p>
            If the crack is wider than about 1/4 in, push in a closed-cell backer rod about 25 percent larger than the crack width so it wedges in snugly. Set it so the sealant depth is roughly half the width of the crack, with a minimum around 1/4 in. On very deep cracks some installers fill the bottom with dry sand first and then add the rod, which saves sealant. The rod does two jobs: it controls depth so you do not pour a whole tube into a bottomless crack, and it keeps the sealant from bonding to the bottom, so it only stretches between the two sides.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Tape the Edges</h3>

          <p>
            Last step before the product: tape the edges if you care about appearance. Painter's tape on both sides of the crack, set about 1/8 in back, gives you a clean line, and you pull it right after tooling. It turns a smeared repair into one that looks deliberate.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="step-by-step" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Step-by-Step: How to Repair Concrete Cracks by Method
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Self-leveling polyurethane in a horizontal crack</h3>

          <p>
            Cut the nozzle at an angle a little smaller than the crack width and load it in a caulk gun with a good thrust ratio; self-leveling sealants are thin but a cheap gun still makes it tiring. Starting at the low end of the crack, fill from the bottom up in a steady pass, letting the sealant flow just to the surface. Self-leveling products seek a level line, so on a sloped driveway they run downhill; work in shorter sections and slightly underfill on slopes, then come back for a second pass once the first skins. Most products skin in about an hour and cure through in several days, depending on depth and temperature.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Gun-grade polyurethane in a wide or vertical crack</h3>

          <p>
            With the backer rod set, push the bead into the joint rather than dragging it over the top, so it fills the full profile against both faces. Then tool it with a rounded spoon, a plastic tooling stick or a wetted putty knife, pressing it into an hourglass profile that is thin in the middle and thick at the bonded edges. That shape is what lets the sealant stretch without tearing. Pull the tape immediately and leave it alone.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Two-part epoxy in a dormant crack</h3>

          <p>
            Epoxy crack fillers usually come in a dual cartridge with a static mixing nozzle, or as two tubs you mix by hand. Mix thoroughly for the time on the label, because unmixed resin stays soft forever. Fill the crack slightly proud of the surface, and if the floor will be coated, grind it flush once it cures. Pot life is short in warm weather, often 10 to 20 minutes, so mix only what you can place.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Patch mortar on broken edges</h3>

          <p>
            Chip out every loose piece until you hit sound concrete, undercut the edges slightly if you can, dampen the area to a saturated surface-dry state, and prime with the bonding agent the manufacturer specifies. Pack the patch in firmly, strike it flush, and match the surrounding finish with a broom or float. Keep it damp for the first day or two if the label calls for it. If a moving crack runs through the patch, leave a groove and fill that groove with flexible sealant afterward, or the crack will simply return through the new mortar.
          </p>

          <p>
            For the hairline case, a liquid crack seal bottle and a penetrating sealer over the whole slab is usually enough. A silane or siloxane penetrating sealer will not fill a crack, but it reduces how much water the slab absorbs, which slows down freeze-thaw damage at the crack edges. See our <a href="/best-penetrating-concrete-sealer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">penetrating concrete sealer</a> picks if you want to do the whole slab after repairs cure.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="curing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Curing, Temperature and Weather Windows
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Temperature</h3>

          <p>
            Temperature decides more repairs than technique. Most polyurethane sealants are labeled for application at about 40 F and rising, and most epoxies want 50 F or higher for the concrete itself, not just the air. Concrete that sat through a cold night stays colder than the air well into a sunny morning. A cheap infrared thermometer pointed at the slab will tell you more than the forecast does.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Best Season to Seal a Moving Crack</h3>

          <p>
            The best time of year to seal a moving crack is a mild day in spring or fall, when the crack is at a middle width. Seal it in the heat of summer, when the slab has expanded and the crack is at its narrowest, and the sealant gets stretched hard all winter. Seal it on a cold winter day, when the crack is at its widest, and summer compresses the bead until it bulges above the surface where tires and shovels catch it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Protecting Fresh Sealant</h3>

          <p>
            Keep rain off fresh sealant for at least the skin time on the label, and ideally a full day. Keep foot traffic off for 24 hours and vehicle traffic off for several days with self-leveling polyurethanes, longer if the joint is deep or the weather is cool. Polyurethane cures by drawing moisture from the air, so very dry conditions slow it down, and very thick beads can take a week or more to cure all the way through.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Curing Cement-Based Patches</h3>

          <p>
            Cement-based patches have the opposite problem. They need water to cure, and a patch that dries out in hot sun and wind in the first day will be weak and dusty. Mist it, cover it with plastic, or use a curing compound as the bag directs. Do not patch when there is a chance of frost within the first 24 to 48 hours unless the product is specifically rated for cold weather.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Before You Seal or Coat the Whole Slab</h3>

          <p>
            If you plan to seal or coat the whole slab after crack repair, wait out the cure times on both labels. A penetrating sealer over sealant that has not cured can discolor it, and a garage floor coating over an uncured filler can lift. Our guide to <a href="/sealing-cracks-in-driveway/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">sealing cracks in a driveway</a> walks through timing the crack work before a full-surface sealer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="call-a-pro" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. When to Call a Pro Instead
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Structural Engineer</h3>

          <p>
            Call a structural engineer, not a contractor, when the crack is in a foundation wall and is horizontal, stair-stepped, wider at one end, wider than about 1/4 in, or paired with bowing, sticking doors or windows, or gaps at the top of the wall. An engineer's visit typically costs a few hundred dollars and is the only opinion with no incentive to sell you a repair. Contractors who sell wall anchors and piers can be excellent, but get the diagnosis from someone who does not install the fix.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Concrete Leveling Company</h3>

          <p>
            Call a concrete leveling company when slabs have sunk, tipped toward the house, or developed trip-hazard offsets. Lifting a slab and filling the void underneath is cheap compared with replacement, and it is the only way to fix the cause of a settlement crack. Our <a href="/concrete-leveling-cost/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">concrete leveling cost</a> guide lays out typical ranges.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Waterproofing Contractor</h3>

          <p>
            Call a waterproofing contractor when a basement crack leaks and keeps leaking after a surface repair. Professional polyurethane injection pushes resin through the full depth of the wall, which a surface caulk cannot do. For floor cracks, our <a href="/basement-floor-crack-repair/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">basement floor crack repair</a> guide explains when water through a slab is a crack problem and when it is a groundwater problem.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When to Consider Replacement</h3>

          <p>
            And consider replacement when more of the slab is cracked than not, when sections are broken into pieces that move independently, or when the surface is spalling and scaling across large areas. Crack repair is maintenance. It is excellent at keeping water out of a sound slab and slowing deterioration. It cannot make a failed slab into a good one, and spending a weekend and a case of sealant on a driveway that needs to come out is the most common wasted effort in this whole category.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How wide does a concrete crack have to be before I should worry?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On a driveway or patio, width alone is rarely alarming; offset and growth matter more. On a foundation wall, cracks wider than about 1/4 in, horizontal cracks, or stair-step cracks in block deserve an engineer's look. A hairline crack that has not changed in a year is almost always shrinkage and only needs sealing to keep water out.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use regular silicone caulk to repair concrete cracks?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is not a good choice. Silicone does not bond as well to concrete as polyurethane, it cannot be painted or sealed over, and most general-purpose silicones are not rated for horizontal traffic. Polyurethane or hybrid polymer sealants labeled for concrete are designed for the movement and abrasion that a driveway or walkway crack sees, and they cost about the same.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I fill control joints in my driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, but only with a flexible sealant, never with mortar or epoxy. Control joints are there to let the slab move and crack in a straight line. Filling them with a rigid material defeats their purpose. A flexible polyurethane over backer rod keeps water and weeds out while letting the joint keep doing its job.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does concrete crack repair last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A well-prepped polyurethane sealant in a moving crack commonly lasts five to ten years before it needs to be cut out and replaced, according to typical manufacturer guidance. Liquid crack sealers in hairline cracks often need touching up every couple of years. Epoxy in a truly dormant interior crack can last as long as the slab itself.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I repair concrete cracks in winter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not outdoors. Most sealants need the concrete at 40 F or above and rising, and cement patches cannot freeze in their first day or two. If a crack is letting water into the base before winter, a temporary fill is better than nothing, but plan to cut it out and redo it properly in spring when temperatures are in the label range.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to grind or route a crack before filling it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not always. Clean cracks with sound, square edges between 1/8 in and 1/2 in can be filled after brushing and vacuuming. Routing helps when a crack is a ragged hairline that sealant cannot get into, or when the faces are crumbly and weak. A crack-chasing blade on an angle grinder turns it into a clean groove sealant can grip.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will crack repair stop a crack from coming back?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It stops water and debris from getting in, which slows down deterioration, but it does not stop the slab from moving. A flexible sealant moves with the crack, so the crack stays sealed rather than disappearing. If the cause is settlement, heave or corroding steel, the crack will widen regardless of the filler until that cause is addressed.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using epoxy on a crack that moves</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Epoxy is stronger than concrete and has almost no stretch. On a crack that opens and closes with temperature, the epoxy holds and the concrete beside it breaks, so you end up with a new crack running parallel to the old one. Save epoxy for dormant, interior cracks and use a flexible polyurethane for anything outdoors that moves.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the backer rod on wide cracks</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Filling a 1/2 in crack full depth with sealant wastes tubes, takes a week to cure, and creates three-sided adhesion: the sealant bonds to the bottom as well as both faces and cannot stretch. It tears in the first cold snap. A closed-cell rod sized 25 percent larger than the gap fixes both problems for a few cents a foot.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing over dirt and dust</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sealant sticks to the first thing it touches. If that is a film of grit or the crumbly skin on old crack faces, the bead peels out in one long strip. Wire-brush, grind if the faces are weak, blow out and vacuum. Ten minutes of cleaning per crack is the difference between one season and many years.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Filling a settlement crack and calling it fixed</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                When one side of the crack sits lower than the other, the base under the slab has moved. Sealant hides the gap for a while, but the slab keeps dropping, the bead tears, and water keeps washing out the base. Lift or level the slab first, then seal the crack as the finishing step rather than the whole repair.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Working in the wrong temperature</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most sealants want the concrete itself at 40 F or above and epoxies usually want 50 F or more. A cold slab can be 10 to 15 degrees cooler than the air on a sunny morning. Applying below the label minimum slows cure, weakens adhesion and invites rain damage before the product skins. Check the slab surface, not the forecast.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

