        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Concrete crack repair works when the product matches the crack. Measure the width, watch it for movement, check for offset and hollow spots, and the choice makes itself: liquid seal for hairlines, polyurethane over backer rod for anything that moves, epoxy for dormant interior cracks, patch mortar for broken edges.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Clean the crack properly and work inside the temperature window on the label, and a sealant repair should last most of a decade. If the crack is offset, growing, rust-stained or in a foundation wall with structural warning signs, fix the cause first. The filler is the last step, not the cure.
          </p>
        </div>

